  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1225 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nFriday's weather continues the trend of triple digit heat, along \nwith low chances for convection off the high terrain to our west.\nHowever, many models depict the upper level ridge beginning to \nweaken slightly late week, allowing the arrival of a weak cold \nfront through the day. This front is slated to provide a very \nminor (but very welcomed) reduction in heat for the weekend, \nmeaning high probabilities (>70%) for temps to stay at or just \nbelow 100 both days. This may also help spark storms for the \nsouthern and western Panhandles Friday afternoon-evneing (20-40% \nPOPs), while the northeast Panhandles are least likely to see rain\nas drier air gradually filters in behind the boundary. \n\nLooking to the first week of August, global guidance shows the upper \nlevel dome of high pressure shifting slightly westward over the \ndesert southwest. At first glance this won't change our weather all \nthat much, with above average temperatures still highly favored each \nday. This pattern does however provide some hope for northwest flow \naloft to bring subtle disturbances that could kick off rain \nopportunities. Trends from GFS ensemble members as well as \naverage precip forecasts slightly hint that we could maintain \noccasional storm chances, but probabilities are overall still \nquite low. With no widespread gullywashers in sight, drought \nconditions will unfortunately persist unless a more significant \nsynoptic pattern change can occur. \n\nHarrel",
